// error
// #4584: an enum value has no member set at all — `parseEnumDecl`
// (compiler/parsertype.bit) accepts only variants in an enum body, and SPEC
// §10.4 puts a method declaration inside a CLASS body and nowhere else — so
// NO name can ever resolve on an enum-typed receiver and every `e.name` on
// one is a certain error. Both shapes used to type-check at rc=0 and be
// refused only by the lowerer, as E0092 ("cannot lower a call whose callee
// does not resolve to a function" / "cannot lower a `.` field access on a
// receiver that is not a class or interface") — a check/build divergence
// (#1430) that also reported at the wrong place. `vMemberJudgeableKind`
// (compiler/validatemember.bit) now admits TypeKind.Enum, so both report
// E0057 at the member NAME, the same diagnostic and span a class receiver
// already got.
//
// The generic receiver is deliberate: `Hold<f64>` is an instantiation, so its
// substituted type is concrete and judgeable. An UNSUBSTITUTED type parameter
// stays permissive (THE SAFETY RULE, `vRecvTypeUnjudgeable`) and is #4627.
// `Color.Red` below is untouched: its receiver is a bare type NAME, which
// `vIsTypeNameRef` returns on before the kind is ever consulted.
enum Color { Red, Green }
enum Hold<T> { Only(T), Empty }

fn main() {
  let c: Color = Color.Red
  let h: Hold<f64> = Hold.Only(7)
  print("${c.nosuchfield}\n")
  print("${h.nosuchmethod()}\n")
}
